ANNEX II
TECHNICAL AND ORGANISATIONAL MEASURES

(In the event of the transfer of sensitive personal data, the technical and organisational measures implemented for such data must be specified separately. No sensitive personal data is intentionally collected; see Annex I.)

A. Access Control and Authentication
- All API endpoints require JWT Bearer token authentication (HS256 algorithm, 30-minute expiry). Unauthenticated requests are rejected.
- Administrative endpoints require a separate admin credential layer enforced by the check_admin_access dependency.
- Database credentials, API keys, JWT secret keys, and all sensitive configuration values are stored exclusively in environment variables (.env file) and are not committed to the source code repository.
- The VPS is accessible only via SSH with public-key authentication. Password-based SSH authentication is disabled.
- Rate limiting is enforced at both the nginx reverse proxy layer and the application layer (slowapi) to mitigate brute-force attacks.

B. Data in Transit
- All client-to-server communication is encrypted using TLS 1.2/1.3 (HTTPS enforced via nginx reverse proxy). HTTP requests are redirected to HTTPS.
- WebSocket connections for LiveKit WebRTC signalling are encrypted via WSS (wss://teqlif.com/rtc).
- Internal service-to-service communication (API server ↔ PostgreSQL, Redis, ClickHouse, MinIO) operates within the private loopback/internal network interface of the VPS.

C. Data at Rest
- PostgreSQL database files are stored on the VPS block storage volume. OS-level filesystem access is restricted to the teqlif system user.
- MinIO object storage (user-uploaded images and videos) is accessible only via authenticated S3-compatible API calls; direct filesystem access requires OS-level credentials.
- User passwords are stored exclusively as bcrypt hashes; plaintext passwords are never stored.
- JWT secret keys and API credentials are stored as environment variables, not in the database.
- Machine-learning preference embeddings are stored as floating-point vectors without linkage to identifiable content.

D. Data Minimisation
- User location data (province and district) is collected at the listing level only, not as a mandatory account-level field.
- AI description generation transmits only listing metadata (title, category, condition, asking price) to external APIs; personal identity data, user location, and all user identifiers are explicitly excluded from all AI API calls.
- ClickHouse analytics events reference internal numeric user IDs only, not e-mail addresses or names.
- Sentry error reports contain internal numeric user IDs and stack traces; personally identifiable fields (name, email) are not explicitly included in error payloads.

E. Content Moderation and Platform Security
- Automated NSFW scoring and image perceptual hashing (pHash) are applied to user-uploaded media to detect policy-violating content.
- Shadow-ban functionality allows silent removal of policy-violating accounts from platform visibility without alerting bad actors.
- The platform applies input validation, SQL injection prevention (ORM-based parameterised queries via SQLAlchemy), and Content Security Policy headers.

F. Data Deletion
- Account deletion: the in-app "Hesabı Sil" flow (password confirmation required) calls DELETE /api/auth/delete-account on the mobile side; the corresponding backend endpoint is not implemented in the current codebase version. Account deletion is currently performed administratively. A user-facing backend implementation of this flow is pending.
- Listings automatically deactivated and left in passive status for 60 days are permanently deleted by a scheduled background task, including removal of associated media files from MinIO storage.
- Device push tokens (FCM, VoIP) are set to null upon account deletion to immediately stop notification delivery.

G. Organisational Measures
- Access to production server credentials is limited to the data controller's authorised personnel.
- Sub-processors are selected on the basis of their own published data protection commitments and, where applicable, standard contractual safeguards applicable under GDPR (European Economic Area sub-processors) or equivalent frameworks (US-based sub-processors).


